<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Limoges</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Augustoritum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Limoges is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> river in west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the historic capital of the Limousin. Founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> as Augustoritum, it took its name from the Gallic Lemovices. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was famed for its enamel work and as a stage on the pilgrimage road to Compostela, and from the 18th century it became the leading centre of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/limoges.jpg" alt="Limoges" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Babsy · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centre-ville_et_champ_de_Juillet.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
